---
description: Put changeable business decisions in named constants
alwaysApply: true
---

# Strategy constants

A value that encodes business logic or strategy, and that a developer may change later, is a named constant. Do not bury it in a condition, a magic number, or a one-off literal.

Put it on the class or file that already owns that area's settings. A developer flips the constant. Do not add an admin control unless someone who does not edit code must change it.

```php
// ❌ BAD — the decision is hidden in the method
if ( $qty <= 3 ) {
	$this->notify();
}

// ✅ GOOD — one place to change the strategy
const LOW_STOCK_THRESHOLD = 3;

if ( $qty <= self::LOW_STOCK_THRESHOLD ) {
	$this->notify();
}
```

Leave literals for loop indexes and for values fixed by a protocol or a third-party API this project does not decide.
